in specifying values could be catastrophically amplified in a far-superior intelli-gence. A super-AI might find unforeseen strategies to achieve its misunderstood objective, undermining hu-man intent.

Yudkowsky’s camp (largely U.S.-based) emphasizes that without a practically perfect defini-tion of our values, a powerful AI will pursue its goals through any means, potentially against human inter-ests [20]. They highlight dangers like instrumental con-vergence – the tendency of any goal-driven agent to seek power, resources, and self-preservation as inter-mediate goals, possibly at odds with our well-being.
In their view, alignment is an almost intractable tech-nical problem, and some argue that advanced AI devel-opment should be paused indefinitely absent a proven solution. While this extreme view is not universal, it underscores the stakes: on one hand, a strict caution-ary (consequentialist) stance aiming to avoid worst-case outcomes;

n-ary (consequentialist) stance aiming to avoid worst-case outcomes;

on the other, the more optimistic (often Eu-ropean) stance that incremental governance and ethical frameworks can guide AI safely.

Compounding the difficulty, recent research has re-vealed that even seemingly aligned AIs can deviate due to complex failure modes. For example, models may engage in mesa-optimization – developing their own emergent sub-goals or internal objectives that diverge from their given goals. An AI that creates a hidden proxy objective might continue to appear aligned with its explicit instructions while actually pursuing a dif-ferent agenda. This can lead to goal misgeneralization, 5 where a system that performed well during training be-haves in unintended ways when encountering novel sit-uations outside its training distribution. In other words, the AI’s learned goal generalizes incorrectly, yielding actions that violate the spirit of its intended objective.

